WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.180
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Brilliant, a problem solving website

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.950
that teaches you to think like an engineer.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:12.850
If we were to define WW2 in just one technology,
the likely winner would be aerial bombing.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:18.010
World War 1 revolved around stagnant and debilitating
trench warfare, that both sides sought to

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:19.789
avoid in future.

00:00:19.789 --> 00:00:25.189
Tanks and planes arose as a way of breaking
enemy lines quickly and inflict damage.

00:00:25.189 --> 00:00:29.550
The rate of technological progress for delivering
larger and more accurate bombs during world

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:35.100
war 2 was astronomical, but early in the war
the technology was still in its infancy.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.750
The Germans relied heavily on the Stuka dive
bomber.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:42.269
A short range bomber whos siren became a harbinger
of death on the battlefield.

00:00:42.269 --> 00:00:46.989
It dived towards its target, planting it firmly
in its crosshairs, imparting its bomb with

00:00:46.989 --> 00:00:51.489
the velocity it need to fly straight to the
target, before releasing and pulling up hard

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:53.780
to avoid a collision with the ground.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:58.690
This procedure was so taxing on its pilot
that the Germans developed an automatic dive-flap

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:03.079
that would deploy even if the pilot had knock
themselves unconscious with g-force.

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:07.659
These tactics arose because bombing aiming
technology was so primitive, bomb sights were

00:01:07.659 --> 00:01:10.530
rudimentary and required skilled operators.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:14.910
Having to factor not only the speed and altitude
of the aircraft, but the terminal velocity

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:19.450
of the bomb and how it would be affected by
wind as it fell.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:23.579
Bombing from high altitude, where the bombers
would be relatively safe from Flak, gave little

00:01:23.579 --> 00:01:26.229
precision to target specific buildings.

00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:30.700
This combined with poor navigation equipment
resulted in both sides devolving into carpet

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.930
bombing, causing massive loss of life to civilian
life, which did little to help either side

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:36.210
win a war.

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:41.150
We saw in a previous episode how much effort
Nazi cities put into anti-aircraft defences,

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.460
and similar efforts were made in Britain.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.280
These tactics did not come cheap, losses were
high for both sides..

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.210
Barnes Wallis, an Aeronautical Engineer at
Vickers, saw the futility in these tactics

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:57.219
and set his focus on developing new technologies
to aid British bombers in destroying strategic

00:01:57.219 --> 00:01:58.719
German positions.

00:01:58.719 --> 00:01:59.770
This was nothing new.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:04.619
Factories, oil storage and transportation
infrastructure were always primary targets,

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:09.820
but Barnes wanted to create a weapon capable
of destroying a target others deemed invulnerable.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:11.010
Dams.

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:13.020
Dams posed a tantalizing target.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:17.790
They supply hydroelectricity, provided water
for industry and the German population, and

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:21.820
the resulting deluge of water from the massive
reservoirs would cause damage much greater

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:24.860
than any bomb the Brit’s had at their disposal.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:28.690
The Germans were not ignorant to the allure
the dams as a bombing target.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:32.580
Attacks on dams had happened earlier in the
war, like the attack on the massive Italian

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:37.780
Tirso dam in Sardinia, which held back Europe’s
largest man-made lake and provided the Italian

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.040
island with a third of its electricity.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:44.090
They attempted the raid in broad daylight
with 8 Swordfish floatplanes.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:48.580
3 simply failed to find the target due to
terrible weather, and the rest were welcomed

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.590
with anti-aircraft fire, taking one of the
planes down.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:55.790
The remaining 4 continued to the target and
dropped torpedoes which travelled underwater

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:59.950
to their target before exploding, but the
dam remained standing.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:03.820
The bombs were simply not powerful enough
to blast through the heavy concrete of the

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:04.820
dam.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:08.880
To make matters worse, all dams across Europe
would be protected by anti-torpedo netting

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.350
from this point on.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:14.980
So a new method to deliver an enormous bomb
capable of taking down a structure this strong

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:16.070
was needed.

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:19.980
Wallis spent most of his time devising devices
to take on challenges like this, and took

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:23.780
several things into consideration when designing
his bombs.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:28.770
Explosive pressures decay extremely quickly,
and thus to ensure damage to the structure

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:33.290
it must be as close as possible, pressure
waves propagate through denser mediums far

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:38.420
more efficiently, and thus explosions underground
or in water will be more powerful.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:43.060
And finally, doubling the size of the bomb
does not result in twice the blast radius,

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:47.680
to increase the blast radius drastically would
require a drastically larger bomb.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:50.090
His design ethos from here was simply.

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:54.660
Design the biggest bomb his planes could carry,
and have them detonate underground or in water

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.230
to maximise their effect even further.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:03.050
He presented his ideas in a 1941 paper “A
Note on a Method of Attacking the Axis Powers”

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:07.540
with the primary focus on a 10 tonne earth
penetrating bomb, that would be dropped from

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:14.010
40,000 feet, this was the most powerful non-nuclear
bomb ever used until just last year, and 42

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:18.000
were dropped in the final year of the war
that helped cut German supplies to the front

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.980
line, by destroying viaducts and railways.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:23.479
But Wallis is remembered for a different bomb.

00:04:23.479 --> 00:04:28.169
The British government hired the Road Research
Laboratory, a civil engineering firm, to begin

00:04:28.169 --> 00:04:32.479
experiments to find the smallest charge needed
to destroy the Mohne Dam.

00:04:32.479 --> 00:04:37.210
They constructed several scale model dams,
and used abandoned dams in Wales to find their

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:38.210
answer.

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:42.250
They found that even a 10 tonne bomb exploding
50 feet from the dam would not destroy it,

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:46.819
but a 2 tonne bomb could do the job if it
was placed directly next to the face of the

00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:47.819
dam.

00:04:47.819 --> 00:04:52.360
With torpedo netting installed, this job required
precision that had not been seen before, and

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:55.270
the method that was devised would break all
convention.

00:04:55.270 --> 00:05:00.110
Barnes decided the best course of action was
to create a skipping bomb, that would bounce

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:04.229
over the protective netting before sinking
next to the dam and exploding.

00:05:04.229 --> 00:05:08.539
He concluded that the mines would have to
impact at less than 7 degrees in order to

00:05:08.539 --> 00:05:13.509
skipp, and all subsequent bounces would also
have to be below 7 degrees to ensure the bomb

00:05:13.509 --> 00:05:15.849
continued until momentum was lost.

00:05:15.849 --> 00:05:19.800
The bomb would be given a backspin prior to
release in order to take advantage of the

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.860
Magnus effect, which is lift created by a
spinning body.

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:27.550
Spinning the bomb provided it stability, like
a bicycle wheel in motion.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:32.060
Helping the bomb remain on a straight trajectory,
but the lift the spinning provided was far

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:33.280
more useful.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:37.990
By adding lift the bomb’s trajectory gained
more horizontal motion, and it’s angle of

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:39.740
impact was reduced.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:43.629
Both of which aid skipping, and allowed the
bomber to release the bomb sooner, giving

00:05:43.629 --> 00:05:46.919
them additional time to escape the imminent
explosion.

00:05:46.919 --> 00:05:50.949
Once submerged the remaining spin would then
help push the bomb forward ensuring it stayed

00:05:50.949 --> 00:05:53.509
in close contact to the dam wall.

00:05:53.509 --> 00:05:57.360
This wholly unconventional bomb posed some
design challenges.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:01.529
Any spinning object needs to be precisely
balanced to prevent vibrations that could

00:06:01.529 --> 00:06:05.110
potential break it, or interfere with it’s
operation.

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:09.490
To aid this, Barnes early spherical designs
were scrapped in favour of a drum which could

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:14.689
be more easily manufactured and balanced,
while also aiding with it’s release mechanism.

00:06:14.689 --> 00:06:18.290
The bombs were fitting to Avro Lancasters,
which were modified to accommodate the bomb

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:23.110
. The bomb doors were removed to fit bomb,
which would protrude below the plane for the

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:24.990
duration of the flight.

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:29.819
The lower ventral guns were removed to reduce
drag, as the mission would be flown so low

00:06:29.819 --> 00:06:32.919
to the ground that they would provide no protection
against fighters.

00:06:32.919 --> 00:06:37.509
Two v-shaped mounting arms were then attached
with free-spinning disc mounts which would

00:06:37.509 --> 00:06:40.199
mate with the support rings on either side
of the bomb.

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:44.919
An off the shelf motor, typically used to
power hydraulic pumps on submarines was used,

00:06:44.919 --> 00:06:46.979
and connected to the bomb with a pulley.

00:06:46.979 --> 00:06:51.189
Finally, to release the bomb Barnes needed
some way of unmating bomb from it’s spinning

00:06:51.189 --> 00:06:52.189
mount.

00:06:52.189 --> 00:06:54.120
This was done rather ingeniously.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:57.999
These mounting arms were stabilised with a
tensioned wire, which kept them firmly mated

00:06:57.999 --> 00:06:58.999
with the bomb.

00:06:58.999 --> 00:07:04.110
When the bomb needed to be released, the tension
was released with a solenoid actuated grip.

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:08.680
Compressed springs would then force the arms
to swing outwards by just a couple of degrees,

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:10.300
enough to release the bomb

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:14.121
The bomb was now ready, but little time was
afforded to the crew that would man the planes

00:07:14.121 --> 00:07:15.250
that would carry them.

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:20.629
A new special squadron was created specifically
for this mission, formed by some of the most

00:07:20.629 --> 00:07:23.530
experienced pilots the RAF had to offer.

00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:28.279
This experience would be needed, as the entire
mission would be flown at extremely low altitude

00:07:28.279 --> 00:07:29.889
at night.

00:07:29.889 --> 00:07:34.100
Navigators had to learn to navigate with limited
information, often little more than the bomb

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:36.690
aimers calling out landmarks they passed over.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:41.210
To increase the time they had to train, their
Lancasters were fitted with blue plastic screens

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:45.089
to the windows to limit visibility and light
during the day.

00:07:45.089 --> 00:07:48.930
After many test drops the crew were finally
informed of their target on the morning of

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:51.289
the attack on May 16th.

00:07:51.289 --> 00:07:56.749
That night they would take off from RAF Scampton
in 3 waves with a total of 19 aircraft.

00:07:56.749 --> 00:07:58.629
Little would go smoothly from here.

00:07:58.629 --> 00:08:02.629
The first of the planes was lost just an hour
into the mission, when it strayed off course

00:08:02.629 --> 00:08:07.039
over the heavily defended Texal Island off
the Northern coast of the Netherlands.

00:08:07.039 --> 00:08:11.590
Two planes collided with power lines and cables
as they flew low over the dutch and german

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:13.029
countryside.

00:08:13.029 --> 00:08:17.469
Another plane flew so low as it crossed the
North Sea, that the low slung bomb collided

00:08:17.469 --> 00:08:22.430
with a wave, dislodging it and forcing it’s
crew to return to base, followed shortly after

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:25.409
by another plane badly damaged by Flak.

00:08:25.409 --> 00:08:29.339
At this point the entire first wave of planes
had failed their mission, but others were

00:08:29.339 --> 00:08:30.910
finally reaching their target.

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:37.000
Gibson, the commander of the squadron, was
the first to arrive flying at 370 km/h just

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:41.650
60 ft from the water, he successfully dropped
his bomb which bounced three times before

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:47.150
sinking and exploding, sending a gigantic
spout of water above the dam, but it had sunk

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:48.840
too short of the dam.

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:53.240
On the following run a bomb bounced over the
dam and destroyed the powerhouse at its base,

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:57.400
while the Lancaster that carried it crashed
into flames after being struck by flak.

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:02.930
Gibson at this point circled back around to
act as a decoy, for Henry Young whos bomb

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:05.340
veered off towards the banks of the reservoir.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:10.330
On the fourth try the squadron finally got
a direct hit, but another blow was needed

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:15.600
to collapse the dam, which came shortly after
as Gibson and Micky Martin flew decoy runs

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:21.870
alongside Maltby who scored a direct hit on
the already crumbling dam, sending 330 million

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:24.400
tonnes of water into the valleys below.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:29.610
With their first target down, the 1st wave
continued on to Eder dam with 3 bombs left,

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.260
which was all they needed.

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:35.320
After two unsuccessful attempts, where one
bomb damaged the plane that was dropping it,

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:39.350
Knight’s Lancaster came in at the perfect
speed and altitude, releasing his bomb before

00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:43.900
quickly pulling hard to avoid the 300 ft hill
directly behind the dam.

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:48.280
The first wave were now out of bombs and made
their journey home, but this leg was just

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:51.800
as dangerous, as the already damaged plane
was shot down soon after.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:55.160
2 more lancasters would also be brought down
by flak on their return leg

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:59.320
The third wave lost two planes before reaching
their newly assigned target.

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:03.491
One strayed over the city of Hamm and was
gunned down by anti-aircraft guns, another

00:10:03.491 --> 00:10:05.290
was lost over the Netherlands.

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:10.280
What remained mounted an unsuccessful attack
on the Sorpe dam, before returning to base.

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:15.291
In all the British lost 8 Lancasters in the
raid, killing 53 men, while another 3 who

00:10:15.291 --> 00:10:18.130
managed to survive their crashes were taken
prisoner.

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:21.200
But the damage inflicted on the Germans was
far greater.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:25.870
The resulting floods wreaked havoc on the
Ruhr valley, every bridge downstream for 45

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:27.190
kilometres was destroyed.

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:30.940
10 factories were destroyed and a further
100 were damaged.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:35.470
Mines were flooded, and an estimated 400,000
tonnes of coal production was lost.

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:41.320
Acres of farmland were wiped out, and over
1500 people died in the resulting floods.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:46.350
All this damage with just 19 aircraft, this
was precision that had not been seen before.

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:50.450
However, some have questioned whether the
raids were worth this loss in life.

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:54.650
Many of the people killed in the floods were
prisoners of war in forced labour, and others

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:59.610
are quick to dismiss this raid as a failure,
because the Germans recovered so quickly.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:04.140
Taking just 5 months to repair the dams, in
time to ensure the reservoirs would fill for

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:05.470
the following summer.

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:09.460
But these people seem to ignore the immense
amount of resources that had to be diverted

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:10.940
to repair the dams.

00:11:10.940 --> 00:11:15.920
They were repaired quickly precisely because
they were so vital to the German war effort,

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:20.590
that it was worth shifting thousands of works
and materials away from the front lines.

00:11:20.590 --> 00:11:24.890
D-Day, which would come just a year after
this raid, could have gone very differently

00:11:24.890 --> 00:11:30.040
if those resources had instead gone on to
fortify the beaches of France even further.

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:34.660
These 19 bombers, pound for pound, did more
damage to the German war effort than any other

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:39.520
British air raid thanks to the precision they
had been engineered to achieve, and precision

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:44.430
would become the focus of many of the wars
greatest engineers once the war had ended,

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:48.230
after all the Moon was much further away than
any previous target.

